BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Building Confidence Through Self-Advocacy and Skills Articulation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260817T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260817T183000
UID:6a3ee3a118f47293771971c3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:We all have valuable skills and experiences\, but can we confi
 dently articulate them? This interactive workshop helps youth participants
  recognize the skills they already have and build the confidence to commun
 icate their value throughout their career journey. Through practical activ
 ities\, participants will learn strategies to articulate their skills and 
 experiences\, strengthen their self-advocacy skills\, and navigate workpla
 ce opportunities effectively.\nThis program is part of TPL's Career Coache
 s in Residence and is generously supported by our lead donor Azrieli Found
 ation along with supporting donors Fidelity Canada\, Friends of Toronto Pu
 blic Library\, South Chapter and\nThe William and Nona Heaslip Foundation.
 \nFor more information\, please visit\ntpl.ca/careercoach
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3ee3a118f47293771971c3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Portfolio Development: Arts Mentor in Residence Series with Elaine
  Chan-Dow
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260723T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260723T163000
UID:6a3d61726db61d280097ead7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:A strong portfolio can help communicate your artistic vision a
 nd open new professional opportunities. In this workshop on portfolio deve
 lopment\, learn strategies for organizing and presenting your work with cl
 arity and cohesion. Participants will explore visual sequencing\, editing 
 techniques and approaches to building portfolios for exhibitions\, grants\
 , freelance opportunities and online platforms.\nThis program will be foll
 owed by a Q&A.\nThis workshop is presented by transdisciplinary artist\,
  educator and researcher Elaine Chan-Dow\, whose art and research practice
  explores culture\, environmental sustainability and contemporary creati
 ve practice.\nLearn more about the\nArts Mentor in Residence\nprogram\, in
 cluding workshops and one-on-one consultations.\nInformation Collection No
 tice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto P
 ublic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, an
 d 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registrat
 ion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles you
 r information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\
 , 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Malvern\, Community Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3d61726db61d280097ead7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Podcasting 101
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260903T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260903T190000
UID:6a3b084c296ea565de2e52c5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Calling all young adults!\nDid you know 'podcast' comes from i
 Pod and broadcast? This intro session will include fun facts about the evo
 lution of podcasting\, an interactive listening component and best practic
 es when making your own podcast. You’ll walk away being able to speak co
 nfidently about the medium and learn about the making of some of your favo
 urite shows. You’ll also have the foundational information needed to sta
 rt your own show!\nNo experience necessary.\nDrop-in!\nHosted by Amanda Cu
 pido\, an award-winning podcast producer\, university instructor\, former 
 TPL Innovator in Residence\, and author of\nLet's Talk Podcasting: The Ess
 ential Guide to Doing it Right\n!
LOCATION:Morningside\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3b084c296ea565de2e52c5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Boba Drink Making Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260820T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260820T184500
UID:6a3582457550c8bf9f5e81d2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this 45 minute Workshop\, participants will learn how to pr
 epare a classic milk bubble tea\, including decorating and mixing techniqu
 es\, as well as the breakdown of core ingredients. A classic lidded cup an
 d straw will be provided to enjoy the boba as you go!\nFood allergy notice
 : Please be aware that food will be used in this program. Food products us
 ed in this program may contain or may have come into contact with common a
 llergens. (*Contains dairy\, sugar\, and caffeine)\nThis program is for\nY
 ounger Adults 18-24\n.\n*Registration Required\nInformation Collection Not
 ice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Pu
 blic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and
  20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registrati
 on\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your
  information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\,
  416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Agincourt\, Main Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3582457550c8bf9f5e81d2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
